WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.930
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:05.580
The word Halloween is derived from "All Hallows'
Eve".

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:11.910
All Hallows' Eve is the eve before the Christian
feast of All Hallows on November 1st.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:16.670
Halloween takes place on the 31st October,
mostly in the western world.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:21.790
More chocolate and sweets are sold on October
28th than any other day of the year.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.010
The most popular of which is Snickers.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.730
Halloween is the second highest grossing commercial
holiday after Christmas.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.690
Americans spend $6 billion on Halloween every
year.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:42.629
But why do we celebrate Halloween?

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:46.219
Halloween started about 2000 years ago in
Ireland.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:50.010
The holiday originated from the Celtic festival
of Samhain.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:55.719
Samhain was celebrated by Celts in Ireland,
the UK and northern France.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:01:00.379
The festival was held to mark the end of the
summer months and harvest time.

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:06.190
The darker, colder months that followed were
associated with death by the Celts.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.900
Samhain began at sunset on October 31st.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.260
They believed this was the time when the ghosts
of the deceased returned to earth, because

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:20.230
the boundaries between the world of the living
and the dead were most blurred.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.000
So why do we call it Halloween today?

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.660
The word Halloween comes from the Christians.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.660
Hallowmas is a three day Catholic holiday
where saints are honoured and people pray

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.290
for the recently deceased.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:38.830
At the start of the 11th century, the Pope
decided that it would last from October 31st

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:44.730
until November 2nd because that's when Samhain
was celebrated and the church was trying to

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.220
convert the pagans.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.280
October 31st, the start of this Christian
holiday is known as All Hallows' Eve, which

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:58.610
over the centuries has been shortened, first
to All Hallow's Even and eventually in the

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.330
18th century to Hallowe'en.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:06.530
The Irish brought their Halloween-like traditions
to the US in 1840.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:11.019
However the commercialised Halloween that
we know today didn't start in the western

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:13.569
world until 1921.

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:19.940
And mass production of Halloween costumes
didn't start until the 1950s, until then Halloween

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.810
costumes were homemade and just plain creepy.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.939
But why do we dress up for Halloween in the
first place?

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:32.159
During the old festival of Samhain spirits
were believed to cross over to the world of

00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:34.760
the living for this one night.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.409
People began to get scared at the thought
of this so they wore handmade masks and odd

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:45.319
clothing in an attempt to disguise themselves
from the spirits so any evil spirits wouldn't

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:46.620
harm them.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.909
Trick or treating is a tradition carried out
across most of Europe, North America and some

00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:52.920
parts of Asia.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.819
But the ritual of dressing up and knocking
on strangers doors in exchange for sweets

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:00.400
differs greatly in some parts of the world.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.260
In France Halloween is seen as an unwanted
American influence and the French rarely trick

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.299
or treat.

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:13.530
In fact the French didn't fully celebrate
the holiday until 1996, because of the French

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.599
Catholic Church's strong campaign against
Halloween.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:23.749
Instead of going house to house asking for
sweets, French children go from store to store.

00:03:23.749 --> 00:03:28.870
In Germany it is customary to put away all
their knives, because they don't want the

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.689
returning spirits to harm themselves.

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:38.349
In Sweden Halloween is known as "Alla Helgons
Dag" and is celebrated for 6 continuous days,

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:40.969
school children are also given a day off.

00:03:40.969 --> 00:03:46.609
In Spanish speaking countries such as Mexico,
Spain and Latin America, Halloween is known

00:03:46.609 --> 00:03:50.639
as "El Dia de los Muertos", The Days of the
Dead.

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:55.480
Families remember their dead relatives by
constructing an altar in their homes, consisting

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.870
of flowers, candy, photographs, water and
samples of the deceased favourite food and

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:02.870
drinks.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.230
But how did the tradition of trick or treating
originate?

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:12.340
It all started in England, across the West
Midlands and Lancashire, in the early 19th

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.169
century, when children would go "souling".

00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:21.209
Kids would go door-to-door, singing songs
and begging for money, food and drink.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:22.870
They would sing songs such as:

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.569
A soul! a soul! a soul-cake!

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:27.020
Please good Missis, a soul-cake!

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:32.120
An apple, a pear, a plum, or a cherry,
Any good thing to make us all merry.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.740
One for Peter, two for Paul
Three for Him who made us all.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.320
The most common treat given out were Soul
Cakes, they were small round cakes with a

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.320
cross on top.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.620
Later in 19th century Britain, souling gave
rise to guising.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:51.200
Where children would dress up and beg for
fruit and money, but instead of singing songs

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.599
to earn these gifts, children would put on
full blown performances such as playing instruments

00:04:56.599 --> 00:04:58.400
and reciting poems.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.289
This tradition eventually gave way to modern
day Trick or Treating when the Scottish and

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:07.009
Irish brought it to North America in 1911.

00:05:07.009 --> 00:05:09.729
But is trick or treating really safe?

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:13.599
What about all those poisoned candy scares
and worried mummies?

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:19.639
Well you can relax, truth is you're more likely
to be ravaged by zombies that receive tampered

00:05:19.639 --> 00:05:20.639
treats.

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:25.620
There have only ever been two documented cases
of people dying as a result of eating Halloween

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.620
goodies.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:32.599
The first was in 1970 when a boy ate a large
amount of heroin that his uncle had hidden.

00:05:32.599 --> 00:05:38.530
The boy's family sprinkled heroin on his halloween
sweets and blamed the sweets instead.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:44.710
The second was when a father poisoned his
own son's sweets to get the compensation from

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:48.580
a life insurance policy he had just taken
out on him.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.090
So what's the lesson here?

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.750
You should probably be more scared of your
parents than strangers.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.860
And finally, why do we carve pumpkins?

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:02.720
In 19th century Great Britain, jack-o-lanterns
where a form of pranksterism, young boys used

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.009
to hollow out and carve faces in turnips to
frighten people on the streets.

00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:09.849
Wait, what, turnips?

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:13.690
That's right before pumpkins, jack-o-lanterns
were created from turnips.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:19.910
And the word "jack-o-lantern", well it comes
from 17th century Britain, when it literally

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:25.300
meant "man with a lantern".

